you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news  on today's programme now the growing  demand for gas around the world  could put global climate targets at risk  as nato troops withdraw from afghanistan  we look at the risks the country is  facing from the climate crisis  and green fingers raising awareness of  green  issues at the hampton court palace  garden festival  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those coming up with the  solutions and we're going to start today  at our data dashboard and take a look at  this  number here now this shows an estimate  of human-induced global warming since  the industrial revolution  in 2015 more than 190 countries signed  the paris agreement  to limit this number to two degrees  preferably  1.5 degrees to do that will require a  huge reduction in the use of fossil  fuels  such as oil and gas but according to the  international energy agency  global demand for gas is set to increase  putting aims to reach net zero emissions  by 2050 at risk well they found that in  2020  global natural gas demand dropped by 1.9  because of an exceptionally mild winter  in the north and the impact  of the covid19 pandemic but demand is  expected to rise by  3.6 this year as lockdowns ease and  economies  recover now part of the increase in  demand is thought to be because of high  temperatures driving demand for air  conditioning  across the northern hemisphere now in  recent weeks parts of the united states  and canada  have experienced record temperatures  while moscow recorded its hottest  ever june day and national records were  also set  in finland belarus and estonia  well the iea is forecasting that unless  major policy changes to curb global gas  consumption are introduced  demand will just keep growing by 2024  they estimate  an overall seven percent rise from  pre-covered levels and that would mean  global targets to reach net zero  emissions by 2050  would be missed well joining me now is  dr aj gambier senior research fellow at  imperial college london's grantham  institute for climate change  and the environment welcome to you it's  easy perhaps to understand why demand  for gas may have dropped during the  pandemic but why is consumption  tipped to rise so much in the years  ahead well i think that  as you were saying there's been this  rebound from the covid  19 pandemic we're seeing a lot of fast  economic growth in the number of regions  and a lot of our energy infrastructure  and assets  are still designed to work off fossil  fuels  now gas is if you like the cleanest of  the fossil fuels of coal oil  and gas but it's still too dirty  if we're to operate in a global economy  which is  trending towards net zero carbon dioxide  emissions which is what we need  to be on track to meet the paris  agreement goal uh of world below two  degrees celsius  and pursuing efforts towards 1.5 degrees  so you know we're seeing this economic  rebound and we're seeing that  uh depending if you like on a lot of gas  energy  but what we really need to do is  transition away from all fossil fuels as  quickly as possible  well yes and where does the onus for  that shift  lie are there some countries that are  more reliant on gas than others  i mean yes you know there are lots of um  countries  across the world in europe and elsewhere  um  that use a lot of gas in their power  generation  uh you know we've seen shifts from coal  to gas  fired power in the united states that  alone has actually been  responsible for a large reduction in  carbon dioxide emissions in the united  states  that might seem like a really good thing  but there's only so far that can go in  terms of moving us towards this net  zero carbon economy so you know there  are lots of countries like that which  have traditionally relied on  dirty or if you like coal-fired power  and are now using gas-fired power  but the real long-term solution comes  from zero carbon power  from renewables like wind and solar and  so on  and every country in the world has the  possibility of using a large share of  renewables  and other low carbon sources so i think  really the responsibility if you like is  in all countries of the world now to  have a clear and  defined pathway to shifting away from  all fossil fuels as quickly as possible  dr aj gambier thanks very much indeed  thank you  in today's other climate news the uk  needs to cut its global environmental  footprint by  75 percent by the end of the decade to  help nature  recover the wwf says the uk's impact on  climate is disproportionately high  but could be reduced through measures  such as reducing food waste and ensuring  all marine resources  come from sustainable sources  these last couple of years we've seen  just extraordinary outbursts of  you know fires around the world you know  the horrible fights we're seeing in  british columbia at the moment  cyprus i saw yesterday but yeah siberia  amazon australia we're seeing these  extreme weather events they those will  become the norms sadly  and we know that we're losing species  around the world as well and  don't forget since 1900 if 500  types of vertebrates have gone extinct  so these are animals no one will ever  see again  and that those risks will increase as if  we don't take action now  jonathan bartley is stepping down from  his role as green  party co-leader his fellow co-leader  sean berry will continue as acting  leader until a leadership election takes  place  bartley is the longest serving leader  the party has had and called his five  years there an  immense privilege  new zealand has experienced its hottest  june in more than a century  figures from the national institute of  water and atmospheric research  show the average temperatures were two  degrees higher than normal  despite a polar blast that hit the  country just last week  the niwa says the weather was a result  of high  sea level air pressure and climate  change  and the campaign to rewild the south  downs national park has been launched  13 000 hectares that's the equivalent of  21 000 football pitches  is set to be restored to its original  condition in order to create more  habitats for plants and animals  it's part of a un-backed initiative to  increase protected areas for nature by  30 percent by 2030.  afghanistan is one of the most  vulnerable countries in the world  to climate change but one of the least  well-equipped to cope with the  impact it may have and as nato troops  withdraw  there are concerns for how the country  will be able to adapt as the climate  crisis  intensifies well in a moment i'll be  speaking to the international rescue  committee's country director in kabul  but first here's a snapshot of some of  afghanistan's climate challenges  [Music]  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  [Music]  well joining me now from kabul is vicky  aiken afghanistan country director at  the international rescue committee  so vikki akin afghanistan extremely  vulnerable to climate change so what do  you think it could mean for the country  well we currently have 12.2 million  people  nearly a third of the population in  emergency  or crisis food insecurity so for example  many of the crops are being destroyed in  the fighting  um there are explosive remnants  of war contaminating fields  and then many of the highways  are blocked by the conflict so if a  farmer is  harvesting fresh produce and has to wait  five days or so to get his food to the  market  then that food is going to be destroyed  and how could climate change  manifest itself there in afghanistan is  the biggest worry  droughts or floods or avalanches how  could it play out  so it plays out all of the above in  different areas of the country we see  severe flooding and in others we see  severe droughts and in some of the areas  like bad geese in the west you see both  the last severe drought was just a  couple of years ago in 2018  and with that drought we saw more than a  quarter of a million people  displaced from their homes we hope this  time  to try to prevent people from leaving  their homes by providing  aid to them where they live um  it was a study done after the last route  showed that it took the average that it  would take the average person  16 years to pay off the debt they  accumulated  during those few months and  you know since then the country has been  devastated by the impact of covet  and the level of people  living under the poverty line has  increased from 50 to over  70 percent vicky aiken thanks very much  indeed  thank you now jobs in green industries  might not  only be good for the environment but for  our pockets too  the think tank onward has found that  industries such as renewable energy  could pay workers 30 more than their  carbon intensive counterparts  but the uk workforce is under prepared  for the transition to net zero  with the report saying more than three  million employees will need to  retrain in specialist skills and  technical qualifications  adam karen batter made the move from  working in oil to renewable energy the  trigger for me to transition at that  point  was more about the decline in the uh  in oil prices back in 2014-15  so i really needed to pivot to a market  that was looking more  healthy and buoyant and by luck i was  given the chance to really start  recruiting into renewable energy  which is now my my uh mainstream  business  oh absolutely it's the one of the best  decisions  uh i've made in my career if not the  best and finally it's a blooming good  year for climate change awareness  at the hampton court palace garden  festival gardens that highlight  efforts to tackle plastic pollution and  the impact of fast fashion  all feature at the event horticultural  hero tom stewart smith's garden  includes a climate resilient meadow  showcasing  plants that grow in hot conditions and  are becoming increasingly common in the  uk  well that's everything from us for today  coming up tomorrow i'll be talking to  actor presenter and wwf ambassador kell  spellman about the first ever great big  green week  you can find out more about that at the  same time here on sky news see you then  you

